  "response": "The collapse is caused by synchronous I/O inside async HTTP handlers. Those calls block the event-loop thread, so concurrent requests queue while CPU remains mostly idle.\n\nRanked by likely contribution:\n\n1. `get_invoice` — `api/routes.py:21–23`  \n   `requests.get()` performs blocking network I/O and `open()` performs blocking file I/O inside an async route. If this is the normal invoice endpoint, it likely has the largest aggregate impact because every request can stall the event loop for the upstream request’s full latency.\n\n2. `export_invoice` — `api/routes.py:31–32`  \n   `subprocess.run()` waits synchronously for a child process, followed by `time.sleep()`, which explicitly blocks the event loop. Each export can freeze unrelated requests. This may be the worst endpoint per invocation, though its overall contribution depends on export traffic.\n\nFix these before addressing any other scan finding:\n\n- Replace `requests` with an async HTTP client such as `httpx.AsyncClient`.\n- Use async-compatible file access, cache/preload the file, or move file work to a thread.\n- Replace `subprocess.run()` with `asyncio.create_subprocess_exec()` or move the export to a background worker.\n- Replace `time.sleep()` with `await asyncio.sleep()`.\n\nThe remaining seven findings do not explain the observed low-CPU latency collapse. The datetime findings affect correctness, the import cycle affects startup reliability, and the large-function/deep-import/naming findings affect maintainability. They should be handled separately after the HTTP hot path is fixed.",
